[17592186109939 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186109940 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186109939 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186109942 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186109939 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186109944 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186109939 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186525247 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186109939 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186525592 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186109939 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186109939 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186109939 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186109942 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186109939 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "b43822f8-5c74-5fd7-8419-7849b452203b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186109940 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{1}{e}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186109940 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186109940 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "8826f4c0-5a42-59a2-9151-b35e6ad20bde" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186109942 #edb/ref 1080 "\\sqrt[3]{e}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186109942 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186109942 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3de17066-fadf-5f18-b644-223597f74318" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186109944 #edb/ref 1080 "e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186109944 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186109944 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "5c270b95-b18d-51d3-bd3a-4c9f0a72b267" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110107 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186525355 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110107 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186525391 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110107 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186525464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110107 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186525528 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110107 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186525590 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110107 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110107 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110107 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186525464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110107 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "90195487-fcb1-57cc-8d0f-33b1c9fdb8ef" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110503 #edb/ref 1069 "e-4146" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110503 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve for $x$ if $\\ln(4x+5)=3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110503 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "44c87021-177d-5f5c-b1f9-47eb08f107a6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110503 #edb/ref 1074 "To remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The logarithm is a natural logarithm, which means its base is $e.$ So, we use a base of $e$ for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4x+5) & =3 \\\\ {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4x+5)} & ={e}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we know that $e^{\\ln(4x+5)} = 4x+5.$ \n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x+5 & ={e}^{3} \\\\ 4x & ={e}^{3}-5 \\\\ x & =\\frac{{e}^{3}-5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110504 #edb/ref 1069 "e-4147" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110504 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the exact solution to $3\\ln (5x+1)-2 = 13.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110504 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0cfb7d21-6ca4-55a3-a46a-7a765ed1b866" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110504 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we rearrange our equation to isolate the logarithmic term, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x+1)-2 & =13 \\\\ 3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x+1) & =15 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x+1) & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the natural logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the natural logarithm is $e,$ so we use a base of $e$ for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x+1) & =5 \\\\ {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x+1)} & ={e}^{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we know that $e^{\\ln (5x+1)} = 5x+1.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x+1 & ={e}^{5} \\\\ 5x & ={e}^{5}-1 \\\\ x & =\\frac{{e}^{5}-1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110505 #edb/ref 1069 "e-4148" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110505 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the values of $x$ such that $\\ln(1-2x) + 5\\ln(1-2x) = 4.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110505 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "85f82353-2436-5e48-984a-5e671ee54024" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110505 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we rearrange our equation by collecting the like logarithmic terms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-2x)+5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-2x) & =4 \\\\ (1+5)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-2x) & =4 \\\\ 6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-2x) & =4 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-2x) & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the natural logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the natural logarithm is $e,$ so we use a base of $e$ for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-2x) & =\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-2x)} & ={e}^{2/3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we know that $e^{\\ln(1-2x)} = 1-2x.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-2x & ={e}^{2/3} \\\\ 1-2x & =\\sqrt[3]{{e}^{2}} \\\\ -2x & =\\sqrt[3]{{e}^{2}}-1 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{\\sqrt[3]{{e}^{2}}-1}{2} \\\\ x & =\\frac{-\\sqrt[3]{{e}^{2}}+1}{2} \\\\ x & =\\frac{1-\\sqrt[3]{{e}^{2}}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110606 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049589 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110606 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049944 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110606 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186110937 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110606 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186110938 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110606 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186233034 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110606 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186233608 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110606 #edb/ref 1039 "Solving Natural Logarithmic Equations" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110606 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186110503 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110606 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "8dec21a9-eec8-5e39-9af6-5ba355a11c5b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110607 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186111396 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110607 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186111397 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110607 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186233040 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110607 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186233041 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110607 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186526005 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110607 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186526009 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110607 #edb/ref 1039 "Solving Natural Logarithmic Equations by First Rearranging the Equation" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110607 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186110504 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110607 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "0ae88a0b-9927-573b-a072-d0aa1861a9cd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110608 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186110882 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110608 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186111369 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110608 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186234259 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110608 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186234262 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110608 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186526100 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110608 #edb/ref 1039 "Solving Natural Logarithmic Equations by Combining Logarithmic Terms" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110608 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186110505 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110608 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "b8c27840-76fa-5c01-98cc-587dc063f19c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110881 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186111508 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110881 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186111509 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110881 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186111510 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110881 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186111511 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110881 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110881 #edb/ref 1045 "Solving Logarithmic Equations Containing the Natural Logarithm" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110881 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186111508 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110881 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048741 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110881 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110881 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "5fbb27e3-c74f-5f9c-91d3-ed4d700e940a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110938 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186107494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110938 #edb/ref 1069 "q-19175" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110938 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110938 #edb/ref 1071 "Find $x$ such that $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x)=5.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110938 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "49019dd1-55bb-562f-a7d6-f6148b65a5e7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110938 #edb/ref 1074 "To remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The logarithm is a natural logarithm, which means its base is $e.$ So, we use a base of $e$ for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x) & =5 \\\\ {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x)} & ={e}^{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x)}=5x.$ \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x & ={e}^{5} \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{5}{e}^{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111369 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186109939 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111369 #edb/ref 1069 "q-66748" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111369 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111369 #edb/ref 1071 "If $\\operatorname{ln}⁡x=1-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$, then what is the value of  $x?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111369 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4428092c-dd91-56c8-97be-17f43bd92085" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111369 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we simplify our equation by collecting the like logarithmic terms, and isolating the logarithm, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =1-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡x+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =1 \\\\ (1+2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =1 \\\\ 3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =1 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The logarithm is a natural logarithm, which means its base is $e.$ So, we use a base of $e$ for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} & ={e}^{1/3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}=x.$ \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={e}^{1/3} \\\\ x & =\\sqrt[3]{e}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111397 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186110107 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111397 #edb/ref 1069 "q-7940" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111397 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111397 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the exact solution to $4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3-7x)+8=24.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111397 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bba3adf3-06bf-565a-834f-7b41da6bd21b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111397 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we rearrange our equation to isolate the logarithmic term, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3-7x)+8 & =24 \\\\ 4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3-7x) & =16 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(3-7x) & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The logarithm is a natural logarithm, which means its base is $e.$ So, we use a base of $e$ for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3-7x) & =4 \\\\ {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3-7x)} & ={e}^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3-7x)}=3-7x.$ \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3-7x & ={e}^{4} \\\\ -7x & ={e}^{4}-3 \\\\ x & =\\frac{{e}^{4}-3}{-7} \\\\ x & =\\frac{3-{e}^{4}}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111508 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186111509 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111508 #edb/ref 1061 14892 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111508 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c10874f0-296d-50d3-83ba-2cd2a1d44b40" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111508 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186111882 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111509 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186111510 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111509 #edb/ref 1061 14893 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111509 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ea3b28d0-0f37-51bc-a6fb-d393b2b79ad1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111509 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186110606 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111510 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186111511 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111510 #edb/ref 1061 14894 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111510 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "058a0098-7255-5e3e-b964-0184ef644aac" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111510 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186110607 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111511 #edb/ref 1061 14895 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111511 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c426a6eb-4605-5f13-aa69-71a65b92078c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111511 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186110608 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111882 #edb/ref 1064 "To solve logarithmic equations containing the natural logarithm $(\\ln),$ we can use the same method as usual: first isolate the logarithm, and then exponentiate both sides of the equation using the same base as the logarithm.\n\nThe key is to remember that the base of the natural logarithm is $e.$\n\nTo demonstrate, let's solve for $x$ in the equation $\\ln x = 2.$\n\nTo remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The logarithm is a natural logarithm, which means its base is $e.$ So, we use a base of $e$ for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =2 \\\\ {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} & ={e}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we know that $e^{\\ln x} = x.$ \n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={e}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution is $x=e^2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111882 #edb/ref 1065 2821 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111882 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "7e032551-9488-5bc5-858a-2197849a42cf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111882 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233608 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186525975 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233608 #edb/ref 1069 "q-19181" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233608 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233608 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the exact solution to $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})=3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233608 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "151a655e-4d18-5ef1-b8cd-5631f4184ad4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233608 #edb/ref 1074 "To remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The logarithm is a natural logarithm, which means its base is $e.$ So, we use a base of $e$ for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{x}{3}) & =3 \\\\ {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x/3)} & ={e}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x/3)}=\\frac{x}{3}.$ \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{3} & ={e}^{3} \\\\ x & =3{e}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525235 #edb/ref 1080 "{e}^{1/5}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525235 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525235 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0e631112-94a1-5d36-a4a3-24d0effe4f62" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525239 #edb/ref 1080 "{e}^{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525239 #edb/r