25975 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186525806 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525975 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186525814 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525975 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186525824 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525975 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525975 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525975 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186525582 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525975 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f6f247dc-e2c5-5442-a20f-ad6c40fb8104" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186526005 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186525954 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186526005 #edb/ref 1069 "q-79393" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186526005 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186526005 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the exact solution to $2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-5x)+3=9.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186526005 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "33c6eec8-2b71-56e5-96cd-913d5dc6b9ab" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186526005 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we rearrange our equation to isolate the logarithmic term, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-5x)+3 & =9 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-5x) & =6 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-5x) & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The logarithm is a natural logarithm, which means its base is $e.$ So, we use a base of $e$ for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-5x) & =3 \\\\ {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-5x)} & ={e}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-5x)}=1-5x.$ \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-5x & ={e}^{3} \\\\ -5x & ={e}^{3}-1 \\\\ x & =\\frac{{e}^{3}-1}{-5} \\\\ x & =\\frac{1-{e}^{3}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186526009 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186525916 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186526009 #edb/ref 1069 "q-110776" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186526009 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186526009 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the exact solution to $2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+1=2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186526009 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3a62752b-0746-55f7-b956-62f3d4e94841" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186526009 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we rearrange our equation to isolate the logarithmic term, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+1 & =2 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1) & =1 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The logarithm is a natural logarithm, which means its base is $e.$ So, we use a base of $e$ for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1) & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)} & ={e}^{1/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)}=x+1.$ \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+1 & ={e}^{1/2} \\\\ x+1 & =\\sqrt{e} \\\\ x & =\\sqrt{e}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186526100 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186525898 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186526100 #edb/ref 1069 "q-110778" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186526100 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186526100 #edb/ref 1071 "If $2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+5\\operatorname{ln}⁡x=7$, then what is the value of  $x?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186526100 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f5e20f20-51b5-534a-8fa4-4e59ce4c58da" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186526100 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we simplify our equation by collecting the like logarithmic terms, and isolating the logarithm, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+5\\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =7 \\\\ (2+5)\\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =7 \\\\ 7\\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =7 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =\\frac{7}{7} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The logarithm is a natural logarithm, which means its base is $e.$ So, we use a base of $e$ for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =1 \\\\ {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} & ={e}^{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}=x.$ \n\nTherefore, $x=e.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
